WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.853
Here are eight numbers.

00:00:01.853 --> 00:00:03.609
Quick: how many are there?

00:00:03.609 --> 00:00:04.892
What's their total?

00:00:04.892 --> 00:00:06.580
Which one is the largest?

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.781
You can answer at a glance.

00:00:08.781 --> 00:00:12.347
But a program can only look at one number at a time.

00:00:12.347 --> 00:00:14.130
So how does it keep track?

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.128
The answer is a handful of loop patterns.

00:00:17.128 --> 00:00:22.360
They're short, but you'll reuse them in nearly every program that processes data.

00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:28.813
This is Lecture Three, part four: loop patterns, and how Python compares values.

00:00:29.163 --> 00:00:34.839
We'll count, sum, filter, search, and find the largest and the smallest.

00:00:34.839 --> 00:00:39.963
Then we'll look closely at double equals, is, strings and floats.

00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:43.493
Every pattern has the same three parts.

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:46.586
Before the loop, set up a variable.

00:00:46.586 --> 00:00:51.679
In the loop, look at one item at a time, and update the variable.

00:00:51.679 --> 00:00:55.283
After the loop, the variable holds the answer.

00:00:56.067 --> 00:01:01.147
To count, start a counter at zero, and add one in every iteration.

00:01:01.497 --> 00:01:03.304
Three: count is one.

00:01:03.304 --> 00:01:05.021
Four: count is two.

00:01:05.021 --> 00:01:06.828
Ninety-eight: three.

00:01:06.828 --> 00:01:11.617
And so on, until after the last item, count is eight.

00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:15.803
To sum, replace add one with add the item.

00:01:15.803 --> 00:01:18.087
The total starts at zero.

00:01:18.437 --> 00:01:26.317
Three, then seven, then one hundred and five, and so on, up to four hundred and thirty-nine.

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:32.854
A variable that collects a result across the iterations is called an accumulator.

00:01:32.854 --> 00:01:38.507
Start it at the value that means nothing yet, and update it once per item.

00:01:39.107 --> 00:01:45.225
For the average, keep both accumulators, and divide the total by the count after the loop.

00:01:45.225 --> 00:01:50.867
Four hundred and thirty-nine divided by eight is fifty-four point eight seven five.

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:56.020
An if inside the loop picks out the items you want.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.068
That's called filtering.

00:01:58.068 --> 00:02:03.787
Here, only values greater than 50 are printed: 80, 111, 99, and 65.

00:02:04.137 --> 00:02:05.789
Is 74 in this list?

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:10.138
Start a Boolean variable, called a flag, at False.

00:02:10.138 --> 00:02:13.617
Set it to True when you find the number.

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:17.886
False, False, False, False, then True at 74.

00:02:17.886 --> 00:02:22.607
And it stays True, because nothing ever sets it back.

00:02:22.957 --> 00:02:28.757
If you want to stop looking once it's found, add a break right after found equals True.

00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:33.286
To find the largest, keep the largest number seen so far.

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:36.973
Each time an item is larger, it becomes the new largest.

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:39.290
Start at -1.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.093
9 beats it.

00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:42.896
39 beats 9.

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:44.699
21 doesn't.

00:02:44.699 --> 00:02:46.010
98 does.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.305
4 and 5 don't.

00:02:48.305 --> 00:02:52.403
100 does, and 65 doesn't.

00:02:52.753 --> 00:02:55.673
After the loop, the answer is 100.

00:02:56.466 --> 00:03:00.680
To find the smallest, flip greater than to less than.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.906
And let's start at -1 again.

00:03:03.256 --> 00:03:05.314
Pause the video and predict.

00:03:05.314 --> 00:03:08.696
What does this program print as its last line?

00:03:09.246 --> 00:03:10.167
After, -1.

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:17.080
No number in the list is smaller than -1, so the smallest is never updated.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.846
And -1 isn't even in the list.

00:03:20.196 --> 00:03:23.318
The same bug hides in the largest program.

00:03:23.318 --> 00:03:27.036
On a list of negative numbers, it would answer -1.

00:03:27.833 --> 00:03:31.846
The fix is to start with no value at all.

00:03:31.846 --> 00:03:35.273
Python's value for nothing is None.

00:03:35.873 --> 00:03:42.589
In the first iteration, smallest so far is None, so the first number, 9, becomes the smallest.

00:03:42.589 --> 00:03:47.233
After that, the usual comparison takes over, and the answer is 4.

00:03:47.583 --> 00:03:49.118
So here's the pattern.

00:03:49.118 --> 00:03:51.909
Start at None, meaning nothing seen yet.

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:57.143
Take the item if nothing has been seen yet, or if it beats the best so far.

00:03:57.933 --> 00:04:02.394
That program tests smallest so far is None.

00:04:02.394 --> 00:04:04.573
So what does is mean?

00:04:04.923 --> 00:04:10.099
A variable is a name that refers to an object in memory.

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:14.998
Double equals asks: do these two have the same value?

00:04:14.998 --> 00:04:18.603
Is asks: are they the very same object?

00:04:19.203 --> 00:04:26.055
Here, a and b are built separately: two list objects with the same contents.

00:04:26.055 --> 00:04:30.563
So a double equals b is True, but a is b is False.

00:04:30.913 --> 00:04:34.058
c equals a doesn't copy anything.

00:04:34.058 --> 00:04:38.157
It makes c a second name for the same list.

00:04:38.157 --> 00:04:39.873
So c is a is True.

00:04:40.223 --> 00:04:47.019
Change the list through c, and the name a shows the change, because it's the same list.

00:04:47.019 --> 00:04:48.503
And b is untouched.

00:04:48.853 --> 00:04:52.882
So compare values with double equals.

00:04:52.882 --> 00:04:59.853
Use is only to test for None, True or False, like: if x is None.

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:06.480
Strings are compared letter by letter, from the left, until two letters differ.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.293
Each character has a number, its code.

00:05:09.293 --> 00:05:13.513
Capital A is 65, capital B is 66, and lower-case a is 97.

00:05:13.863 --> 00:05:21.505
So hello is greater than Bye, because lower-case h is 104, and capital B is only 66.

00:05:21.505 --> 00:05:26.783
Every capital letter comes before every lower-case letter.

00:05:27.133 --> 00:05:34.322
And the string "10" is less than the string "9", because the character '1' comes before the character '9'.

00:05:34.322 --> 00:05:38.053
Convert typed numbers with int before you compare them.

00:05:38.403 --> 00:05:43.166
And when one string runs out first, the shorter one is smaller.

00:05:43.166 --> 00:05:45.283
So cat is less than catalog.

00:05:46.066 --> 00:05:48.963
What about values of different types?

00:05:48.963 --> 00:05:53.113
An integer and a float with the same value are equal.

00:05:53.113 --> 00:05:55.306
7 double equals 7.0 is True.

00:05:55.656 --> 00:05:59.057
But a number and a string are never equal.

00:05:59.057 --> 00:06:01.730
7 is not equal to the string "7".

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:06.912
And asking whether the string "7" is less than 8 is a TypeError.

00:06:06.912 --> 00:06:08.856
Python refuses to guess.

00:06:09.633 --> 00:06:12.724
Last, floating-point numbers are approximate.

00:06:12.724 --> 00:06:19.113
0.1 can't be stored exactly in binary, just as one third can't be written exactly in decimal.

00:06:19.463 --> 00:06:26.859
Add 0.1 to itself ten times, and you get 0.999, and so on.

00:06:26.859 --> 00:06:27.625
Not 1.

00:06:27.625 --> 00:06:32.343
And 0.1 plus 0.2 is not equal to 0.3.

00:06:32.693 --> 00:06:36.186
So don't compare floats with double equals.

00:06:36.186 --> 00:06:43.333
Ask whether they're close enough: is the absolute difference smaller than a tiny number?

00:06:43.933 --> 00:06:46.013
This matters in loops too.

00:06:46.013 --> 00:06:52.253
A while loop that waits for x to equal 1.0, adding 0.1 each time, never stops.

00:06:52.253 --> 00:06:55.773
x jumps from just under one to just over it.

00:06:56.566 --> 00:07:02.504
When an AI assistant writes a loop like these for you, check its starting values.

00:07:02.504 --> 00:07:11.886
For every accumulator or so-far variable, ask what happens on an empty list, a list of one item, and a list of negative numbers.

00:07:12.236 --> 00:07:12.715
Try it.

00:07:12.715 --> 00:07:20.787
Ask an assistant for a program that reads numbers until the user types done, then prints the largest and the smallest.

00:07:20.787 --> 00:07:23.865
Does it work if the first number is negative?

00:07:23.865 --> 00:07:26.396
If the user types done straight away?

00:07:27.166 --> 00:07:28.366
Let's sum up.

00:07:28.716 --> 00:07:38.956
The loop patterns are count, sum, average, filter, search with a flag, and largest or smallest so far.

00:07:39.306 --> 00:07:46.346
Start a so-far variable at None, not at a made-up number, and test it with is None.

00:07:46.696 --> 00:07:51.445
Double equals compares values, and is compares identity.

00:07:51.445 --> 00:07:55.176
And never compare floats with double equals.

00:07:55.526 --> 00:07:57.613
Here's one to try.

00:07:57.613 --> 00:08:09.668
Given the list [12, 7, 3, 20, 15, 8], write one loop that counts the even numbers and sums the odd ones.

00:08:09.668 --> 00:08:12.566
It should print 3 and 25.

00:08:13.116 --> 00:08:15.047
That's the end of Lecture Three.

00:08:15.047 --> 00:08:20.116
In Lecture Four, we'll package code into functions, and work with strings and files.

